in @prism

# @epistemologic/pact keyword bindings — the substrate-decl'd shape
# of the `bilateral <name> { sentinel "..." arity <n> require ... }`
# block.
#
# Companion to shards/epistemologic/pact/bilateral.mirror (which
# declares the @epistemologic/pact/bilateral prism + the typed record
# carrier + the reflective evaluator action). The bootstrap's keyword
# harvester (bootstrap/src/grammar.rs::merge_keyword_sources +
# companion_keyword_sources) reads `<op> <keyword>` 2-word lines
# inside a `grammar { ... }` block to build the tokenizer's keyword
# table; this file is the substrate-altitude declaration of those
# bindings for the bilateral shape.
#
# Same precedent as shards/mirror/spec/keywords.mirror (companion to
# @mirror/spec) and shards/song/keywords.mirror (companion to @song),
# tracked by `companion_keyword_sources` in bootstrap/src/grammar.rs.
#
# Without these bindings, the tokenizer would surface every bilateral
# block — `bilateral name { sentinel "..." arity 1 }` — as a Dark
# region (unknown construct with a `{ ... }` body), and the AST walker
# would have nothing structural to walk. The bindings let the existing
# tokenize+parse infrastructure produce a typed AST that the reflective
# `apply_h::act` evaluator (Reed follow-up per
# docs/specs/bilateral-predicate-substrate-shape.md §5.3) walks
# directly, retiring the ~30 hand-typed `if action == "X" &&
# arg.oid.contains("Y")` arms in bootstrap/src/apply_h.rs.
#
# === Grammar-decl'd registration (Reed follow-up tick) ===
#
# Per docs/specs/bilateral-predicate-substrate-shape.md §5.3 step 2:
# Reed adds ONE line to `bootstrap/src/grammar.rs::companion_keyword_
# sources` mapping this file as a companion source for
# `shards/mirror/grammar.mirror`. That's the ONLY Rust change the
# retirement contract requires beyond the reflective evaluator itself
# (~50 LOC in apply_h.rs).
#
# === Kinds chosen so the tokenizer's existing per-kind path produces
# === the right shape:
#
#   focus    — opens a recursively-scanned brace block. Top-level
#              `bilateral NAME { ... }` uses this so the four inner
#              directives (sentinel / arity / require) parse as child
#              AST nodes of the bilateral declaration.
#              Same precedent as `focus project` / `focus target` /
#              `focus cli` in shards/mirror/spec/keywords.mirror.
#
#   project  — single-line directive that consumes one identifier /
#              string-literal / integer as its `name` field, no brace
#              block. Used for `sentinel "..."` / `arity <n>` /
#              `require <ref>`.
#              Same precedent as `project source` / `project altitude`
#              / `project emit` in shards/mirror/spec/keywords.mirror.
#
# These bindings are the dispatch surface the bootstrap's reflective
# evaluator will read; bilateral.mirror's typed record carrier + the
# `discharge(decl, args) -> verdict { \ }` action ARE the substrate's
# settled declaration of what the shape MEANS. Both surfaces coexist
# by design — the keyword bindings are the grammar-loader's parse
# surface; the shard-decl is the substrate's semantic surface.

grammar @epistemologic/pact("bilateral") {
  # === bilateral block (recursively scanned body) ===
  #
  # Top-level `bilateral <name> { ... }` block declaring a bilateral
  # predicate that dispatches reflectively via apply_h::act.
  focus bilateral

  # === bilateral fields (single-line directives inside the block) ===
  #
  # `sentinel "<byte-string>"` — the content-addressed witness the
  # predicate checks for containment of in each argument's ref.oid.
  # Non-empty substrate-invariant. Value is the double-quoted string.
  project sentinel

  # `arity <nat>` — the number of arguments the predicate takes.
  # 1 for base bilaterals; 2 for two-witness composed pairs; N for
  # N-way composed with `require` populated.
  project arity

  # `require <bilateral-ref>` — optional; when composed, names one
  # sub-bilateral this composition AND-conjuncts over. Multiple
  # `require` lines allowed inside one bilateral block; the reflective
  # evaluator recursively evaluates each and returns Fail on first
  # non-Pass.
  project require
}
